\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Does Participating in a Band Ensemble Improve Academic Performance?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 10, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the most recent recession, there have been decreases to public funding
 across the board.
 Even though education is the last budget areas to be cut, many universities
 are dealing with declines in both state and federal funding, as well as
 rising costs.
 Despite the recession ending in 2009, many districts will be unable to
 regain their pre-2008 funding until several years from now.
 Consequently, many universities are forced to make difficult decisions
 in order to balance their budgets while still meeting the needs of students.
 Since extracurricular activities, such as music performance ensembles,
 are considered 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

non-essential,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 they have faced some of the deepest budgetary cuts and restrictions.
 However, many opponents of these cuts claim that reducing or eliminating
 these particular activities does more harm to the students than school
 administrators anticipate.
 In fact, there is a strong correlation between involvement in a band ensemble
 and positive performance in school.
 Yet, as we know, correlation does not imply causation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With my project, I intend to measure the effect participating in a band
 ensemble, most notably marching band, has on academic performance.
 Marching band is a unique treatment to analyze because it requires physical
 exertion, as well as musical aptitude.
 In regards to academic performance, I can measure the effect on both GPA
 and retention rate, which are two important outcomes to university leaders.
 The notable problem facing this examination is the fact that the treatment
 variable is endogenous.
 We cannot rule out the fact that participating in marching band may be
 correlated with some omitted variable, such as innate ability, which also
 affects outcomes.
 To solve this problem, I plan to employ a random discontinuity approach.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To explain, close to 600 students try out for the marching band at The Florida
 State University.
 Students are graded on both their marching and playing ability, and they
 are given scores in both categories.
 After a week long audition process, final scores are tabulated and the
 directors set a cutoff.
 Typically, anyone above the cutoff makes the band, and anyone below does
 not.
 I know that the necessary identifying assumption for a regression discontinuity
, i.e., all other determinants of the outcome vary smoothly across the threshold,
 is likely to hold.
 Individuals cannot manipulate where they are relative to the cutoff because
 known of them know what the cutoff is.
 In fact, the band directors do not set the cutoff until they observe the
 entire distribution of scores for a particular section.
 In addition, it is difficult to imagine anything else besides treatment
 changing at the cutoff.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, depending on the focus of my treatment variable, this can either be
 a fuzzy or a sharp RD.
 To elaborate, suppose I consider the cutoff to be the primary determinant
 of whether or not a student is in a band ensemble in general.
 Yet, the students who fail to make the marching band still have the opportunity
 to join other band ensembles on campus, as well as those who make the band.
 So, I have a situation in which there are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

always-takers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

never-takers.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 As such, I would only obtain a local average treatment effect for compliers
 at the end of my analysis.
 However, if I just consider the effect of marching band on outcomes, then
 I believe I will have a sharp RD because the number of students above the
 cutoff who elect not to join the band is very small.
 Plus, my estimate will be an average treatment effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If I decide to go with the former route, then I may run into problems with
 my exclusion restriction.
 I cannot say with certainty that the only effect being above or below the
 cutoff has on outcomes is through participation in marching band because
 as I stated earlier, those who fail to make the marching band are more
 likely to join another band ensemble on campus.
 However, I can attempt to estimate the effect joining another band ensemble
 may have on outcomes.
 Music majors who are participants in the marching band are also required
 by their degree program to be members of other band ensembles.
 If being in another band ensemble has a positive effect on outcomes, then
 this probably holds true for all members of the marching band.
 As a result, I can choose to ignore the effect being in other ensembles
 altogether, which causes my estimates to be biased downwards.
 In turn, this will give me a conservative lower bound estimate on the effect
 of band participation on outcomes.
 Lastly, the reduced form equation, i.e., the effect of marching band on outcomes,
 is still a topic of interest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In regards to the data, I will try to obtain outcome data from the university's
 registrars office.
 If I am unable to collect exact data on grades, then my outcome variable
 of interest will be the retention rate of students from freshman to sophmore
 year.
 This outcome is still an area of concern for univsersities because the
 attrition rate of students between freshman and sophmore year is highest
 compared to the additional years spend in college.
 In the long run, it is important for universities to retain as many students
 as possible from year to year.
 Plus, I am fairly certain I will be able to gather information on whether
 on not freshman students who tried out for the marching band elected to
 leave the university following their first year in school.
 Finally, total audition and cutoff scores are collected every year during
 the audition process, so I know this data is available.
\end_layout

